sec.p:
In our experiment, the bacterial protocell grew in the quasi-2D interstitial space between the double-layered agar pad and the multi-well culture plate. Taking a cell as a “pancake” with area A(t) and thickness h, the surface area of a cell can be approximated by S(t) = 2A(t) + P(t) · h and the cell volume V(t) = A(t) · h, where P(t) is the perimeter of the cell area A(t) and it can be calculated from the cell profiling results described above. Both S(t) and V(t) are assumed to follow exponential growth dynamics, i.e., S(t) = S0 exp(α t) and V(t) = V0 exp(βt), where S0 and V0 are the initial cell’s surface area and volume, and α and β are the surface area growth rate and volume growth rate, respectively; note that the molecular machinery for phospholipid synthesis is primarily located on the cell membrane [50], so the speed of cell membrane synthesis is expected to be proportional to cell surface area, i.e., dS(t)/dt ∝ S(t), resulting in the exponential growth dynamics. We introduced two geometrical parameters, the circularity C=4π⋅A(t)P(t)2\begin{document}$C=\frac{4 \pi \cdot A(t)}{P(t)^{2}}$\end{document} and the effective radius Reff(t)=A(t)π\begin{document}$R_{e f f}(t)=\sqrt{\frac{A(t)}{\pi}}$\end{document}, and substituted A(t) and P(t) with the two parameters in the expressions of S(t) and V(t). Thus, we have the ratio between dS(t)/dt and dV(t)/dt for protocell growth (denoted as η1(t)):
(1.)
η1(t)=[dS(t)dt]/[dV(t)dt]=α(2πReff2(t)+2πReff(t)C(t)⋅h)β(πReff2(t)⋅h)=2αβ(1h+1Reff(t)C(t)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} \eta_{1}(t)=\left[\frac{d S(t)}{d t}\right] /\left[\frac{d V(t)}{d t}\right]=\frac{\alpha\left(2 \pi R_{e f f}^{2}(t)+\frac{2 \pi R_{e f f}(t)}{\sqrt{C(t)}} \cdot h\right)}{\beta\left(\pi R_{e f f}^{2}(t) \cdot h\right)} \\ =\frac{2 \alpha}{\beta}\left(\frac{1}{h}+\frac{1}{R_{e f f}(t) \sqrt{C(t)}}\right) . \tag{1.} \end{gather*}$$\end{document}
For a perfect circular disk of the same volume (i.e., with radius Reff(t) and thickness h) and undergoing the same volume growth dynamics, the ratio between its surface area growth speed and volume growth speed η0(t) is:
(2.)
η0(t)=2h+1Reff(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \eta_{0}(t)=\frac{2}{h}+\frac{1}{R_{e f f}(t)} . \tag{2.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We defined the surface-volume balance ratio η (t) as follows:
(3.)
η(t)=η1(t)/η0(t)=2αβ(1h+1Reff(t)C(t))2h+1Reff(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \eta(t)=\eta_{1}(t) / \eta_{0}(t)=\frac{\frac{2 \alpha}{\beta}\left(\frac{1}{h}+\frac{1}{R_{\text {eff }}(t) \sqrt{C(t)}}\right)}{\frac{2}{h}+\frac{1}{R_{\text {eff }}(t)}} . \tag{3.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Higher values of η indicate that cells are more capable of keeping surface-volume balance; in particular, η < 1 indicates that the current surface area growth speed is not sufficient to keep up with the cell’s volume increase, even if the cell is approaching a circular disk shape that would require minimal surface area growth. To compute the η curves in Fig. 2A, we calculated the growth rates of surface area and volume of individual cells (α and β) by fitting the experimentally obtained temporal dynamics of S(t) and V(t) into exponential functions (using the least-squares method in MATLAB) in the form of S(t) = S0 exp(αt) and V(t) = V0 exp(βt); we also calculated their Reff(t) and C(t) based on the single-cell area tracking data obtained by the procedures described above. The cell thickness h was chosen to be 0.8 µm, equivalent to the width of walled cells. The colormaps of η in Fig. 2B and Fig. 2C were obtained with Eq. 3 (substituting the single-cell growth rates of surface area and volume α and β in the equation with their ensemble average) for cells in protocolonies and for isolated protocells, respectively.

The global energy ℋ in our model consists of boundary energy ℋBoundary, area free energy ℋArea, perimeter free energy ℋPerimeter and a substrate-pining energy ℋ Substrate:
(5.)
H=HBoundary+HArea+HPerimeter+HSubstrate. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{H}=\mathcal{H}_{\text {Boundary }}+\mathcal{H}_{\text {Area }}+\mathcal{H}_{\text {Perimeter }}+\mathcal{H}_{\text {Substrate }} . \tag{5.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The boundary energy is described as follows:
(6.)
HBoundary=∑ı→∑ȷ→∈neighborhood ofı→Jτ(σ)τ(σ′)[1−δ(σ(ı→),σ′(ȷ→))], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{H}_{\text {Boundary }}=\sum_{\vec{\imath}} \sum_{\vec{\jmath} \in \text { neighborhood of } \vec{\imath}} J_{\tau(\sigma) \tau\left(\sigma^{\prime}\right)}\left[1-\delta\left(\sigma(\vec{\imath}), \sigma^{\prime}(\vec{\jmath})\right)\right], \tag{6.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where J is the contact energy (see Table S1), (l⇀\begin{document}$\stackrel{\rightharpoonup}{\boldsymbol{l}}$\end{document}is a pixel position of spin σ,ȷ→\begin{document}$\vec{\jmath}$\end{document} is the Moore neighborhood (8-connected neighborhood) of (l⇀\begin{document}$\stackrel{\rightharpoonup}{\boldsymbol{l}}$\end{document}, and τ (σ) is the type of spin σ. The δ is the Kronecker delta function:
(7.)
δ(σ,σ′)={0,(σ≠σ′)1,(σ=σ′) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \delta\left(\sigma, \sigma^{\prime}\right)=\left\{\begin{array}{l} 0,\left(\sigma \neq \sigma^{\prime}\right) \tag{7.}\\ 1,\left(\sigma=\sigma^{\prime}\right) \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
Note that J is set to be inversely related to the distance between neighboring lattices, in order to reduce lattice anisotropy due to the unequal distance of lattice sites in the 8-connected neighborhood in the square lattice. Experimentally, a cell cannot lose its area without cell-cell interactions unless lysis happens; therefore, the modeled cell occupied pixel (τ = 1) was set to reject the replacement by the environment (τ = 0).

The area free energy was used to calculate the penalty for deviations of the current area from the target area; here the area corresponds to the volume of cells in 3D but not the membrane area. The function of ℋArea is adapted from the reference [31]:
(8.)
HArea=∑σ[κAσ−Rloge⁡(Aσ+AB)−H0], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{H}_{\text {Area }}=\sum_{\sigma}\left[\kappa A_{\sigma}-R \log _{\mathrm{e}}\left(A_{\sigma}+A_{\mathrm{B}}\right)-\mathcal{H}_{0}\right], \tag{8.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(9.)
H0=R−κAB−Rln⁡(Rκ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{H}_{0}=R-\kappa A_{B}-R \ln \left(\frac{R}{\kappa}\right) . \tag{9.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In Eq. 8 Aσ is the current area of cell σ (with σ > 0) and AB is a buffering area. K and R are parameters that control the shape of the function, and ℋ0 is a value that shift the minimum of this function. Eq. 8 is different from the commonly used quadratic effective energy to describe volume conservation; it includes an osmotic pressure contribution [31] that prevents cell volume collapse during cell compression.

Nonetheless, denoting the target area of cell σ as Aσ0(Aσ0≡−AB+Rκ)\begin{document}$A_{\sigma}^{0}\left(A_{\sigma}^{0} \equiv-A_{B}+\frac{R}{\kappa}\right)$\end{document}, which can be found by minimizing the function [κAσ− R loge(Aσ+ AB)], one can expand ℋArea near Aσ0\begin{document}$A_{\sigma}^{0}$\end{document} and obtain an approximate expression for ℋArea to recover a quadratic effective energy:
(10.)
HArea≃∑σλA(Aσ−Aσ0)2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{H}_{\text {Area }} \simeq \sum_{\sigma} \lambda_{A}\left(A_{\sigma}-A_{\sigma}^{0}\right)^{2}, \tag{10.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(11.)
λA≡2(κ)2R. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{A} \equiv \frac{2(\kappa)^{2}}{R} . \tag{11.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here λA is referred to as the weight of volume growth. The target area follows exponential growth as described below:
(12.)
dAσ0dt=βVAσ0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d} A_{\sigma}^{0}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=\beta_{\mathrm{V}} A_{\sigma}^{0} \tag{12.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where βV is the cell volume growth rate. Note that Eq. 8 but not Eq. 10 was used for the calculation of the area free energy.

The membrane tensile strain (Fig. 5C,E) was calculated at the end of each MCS. The membrane tensile strain of a cell was proxied by the difference between its current membrane area and its predicted membrane area at the current time. Assuming that the cells have a “pancake” geometry as in experiments, the membrane area of the σ-th cell, denoted as Sσ, consists of two bases of area Aσ and a lateral surface of height h. Thus, the total membrane area is:
(17.)
Sσ=2Aσ+h⋅Pσ, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S_{\sigma}=2 A_{\sigma}+h \cdot P_{\sigma}, \tag{17.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Pσ is the perimeter of the σ-th cell. The predicted membrane area Sσ0\begin{document}$S_{\sigma}^{0}$\end{document} is:
(18.)
Sσ0=2Aσ0+h⋅Pσ0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S_{\sigma}^{0}=2 A_{\sigma}^{0}+h \cdot P_{\sigma}^{0}, \tag{18.} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the Aσ0\begin{document}$A_{\sigma}^{0}$\end{document} and Pσ0\begin{document}$P_{\sigma}^{0}$\end{document} are the target area and target perimeter that evolves according to Eq. 12 and Eq. 14, respectively. For isolated cells, we compared Sσ and Sσ0\begin{document}$S_{\sigma}^{0}$\end{document} to estimate the overall membrane tensile strain (Fig. 5C,E, green data points), which is defined as follows:
(19.)
ε={Sσ−Sσ0Sσ,Sσ>Sσ00,Sσ≤Sσ0. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \varepsilon=\left\{\begin{array}{ll} \frac{S_{\sigma}-S_{\sigma}^{0}}{S_{\sigma}}, & S_{\sigma}>S_{\sigma}^{0} \tag{19.}\\ 0, & S_{\sigma} \leq S_{\sigma}^{0} \end{array} .\right.$$\end{document}
For cells in protocolonies, the membrane tensile strain is computed for the periphery area and the bulk part separately by replacing Sσ(Sσ0)\begin{document}$S_{\sigma}\left(S_{\sigma}^{0}\right)$\end{document} in Eq. 19 with Pσ(Pσ0)\begin{document}$P_{\sigma}\left(P_{\sigma}^{0}\right)$\end{document} or Aσ(Aσ0)\begin{document}$A_{\sigma}\left(A_{\sigma}^{0}\right)$\end{document}, respectively. For simplicity, in all simulations for the membrane tensile strain calculation, we turned off cell lysis.
